<section class="lsec rv " id="why" aria-labelledby="h-why"><!-- DYNAMIC: $landing->sections[0] (split) -->
 <div class="lw"><p class="eyb"><span>01 · Why Laravel</span></p><h2 id="h-why">Why Laravel for Business Applications</h2><div class="split"><div><p class=lead>When you need software that handles real revenue, customer data and daily operations, not a weekend prototype, Laravel is the framework I reach for on every serious build. Its built-in authentication, encryption, CSRF protection and validation system give CRM and SaaS products a secure foundation from day one.</p><p>For teams in Karachi and across Pakistan scaling toward international clients, this security baseline matters: one breach in a payroll or lead system can cost more than the entire development budget.</p><p>Laravel’s ecosystem (Eloquent ORM, queued jobs, event broadcasting and first-class REST API tooling) lets me architect multi-tenant platforms and high-volume backends without reinventing infrastructure. Packages for permissions, auditing and API tokens integrate cleanly, so I spend time on your business logic, not plumbing.</p></div>
 <aside class="wbox"><small>Built in, from day one</small><ul class="pills"><li>Authentication</li><li>Encryption</li><li>CSRF protection</li><li>Validation</li><li>Eloquent ORM</li><li>Queues</li><li>Broadcasting</li><li>REST APIs</li></ul><p>Whether you’re a Karachi startup launching a subscription product or an enterprise replacing spreadsheets with a custom portal, Laravel scales from MVP to production load with predictable upgrade paths.</p></aside></div></div>
</section>

<section class="lsec rv " id="services" aria-labelledby="h-services"><!-- DYNAMIC: $landing->sections[1] (cards) -->
 <div class="lw"><p class="eyb"><span>02 · Services</span></p><h2 id="h-services">Laravel Development Services I Offer in Karachi</h2><p class="lead nar">I focus on backend systems that power revenue, not generic brochure sites. These are the core services I deliver for local businesses and remote clients who need a proven Laravel developer in Pakistan with real enterprise experience.</p>
 <div class="sgrid"><article class="sv"><span class="fi"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M3 5h18l-7 8v6l-4-2v-4z"/></svg></span><span class="nn">01</span><h3>Custom CRM backends</h3><p>Lead routing engines, pipeline automation, role-based dashboards and reporting layers tailored to your sales and operations workflow. Every module is built for your team’s actual process, not a one-size-fits-all template.</p></article><article class="sv"><span class="fi"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d="m8 8-5 4 5 4M16 8l5 4-5 4M14 5l-4 14"/></svg></span><span class="nn">02</span><h3>SaaS API architecture</h3><p>Subscription-ready backends with tenant isolation, webhook handlers and versioned JSON endpoints, so your web app, mobile clients and third-party integrations share one reliable Laravel REST API layer.</p></article><article class="sv"><span class="fi"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="3" y="3" width="8" height="8" rx="2"/><rect x="13" y="3" width="8" height="8" rx="2"/><rect x="3" y="13" width="8" height="8" rx="2"/><rect x="13" y="13" width="8" height="8" rx="2"/></svg></span><span class="nn">03</span><h3>Multi-tenant systems</h3><p>Separate data per client or branch while sharing infrastructure: ideal for agencies, franchise networks and B2B platforms that need clean tenant boundaries without running separate servers per customer.</p></article><article class="sv"><span class="fi"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="3" y="4" width="18" height="16" rx="2"/><path d="M3 9h18M8 13h5M8 16h8"/></svg></span><span class="nn">04</span><h3>Admin portals</h3><p>Internal dashboards with granular permissions, audit trails and bulk actions, so managers control data without touching raw databases or waiting on developer tickets for every report.</p></article><article class="sv"><span class="fi"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="6" cy="12" r="2.5"/><circle cx="18" cy="6" r="2.5"/><circle cx="18" cy="18" r="2.5"/><path d="m8.2 11 7.6-4M8.2 13l7.6 4"/></svg></span><span class="nn">05</span><h3>REST API development</h3><p>Documented, authenticated endpoints for mobile apps, partner integrations and headless frontends, with rate limiting, validation and consistent error handling built in from the start.</p></article><article class="sv"><span class="fi"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 20V5M6 11l6-6 6 6"/><path d="M4 20h16"/></svg></span><span class="nn">06</span><h3>Legacy Laravel upgrades</h3><p>I migrate aging PHP/Laravel codebases to modern PHP 8+ and current Laravel releases, fixing security gaps, improving query performance and preparing your app for long-term maintenance without a full rewrite.</p></article></div></div>
</section>

<section class="lsec rv dark" id="audit" aria-labelledby="h-audit"><!-- DYNAMIC: $landing->sections[2] (audit) -->
 <div class="lw"><p class="eyb"><span>03 · Takeover</span></p><h2 id="h-audit">Code Audit, Takeover &amp; Legacy Migration</h2><div class="aud"><div><p class="lead">A large share of Laravel work isn’t building something new; it’s inheriting someone else’s codebase. Before touching anything, I run a structured audit: dependency versions and security patches, N+1 query problems, missing database indexes, untested critical paths, and whether the code follows Laravel’s conventions or fights them at every turn.</p><div class="rep"><b>What you get</b><p>You get a written report of what’s actually wrong, ranked by risk, before any rebuild work starts. So you’re not paying for a rewrite you didn’t need, or missing a real problem a surface-level look wouldn’t catch.</p></div></div>
 <div class="chk"><small>Audit checklist</small><ul><li><span>✓</span>Dependency versions &amp; security patches</li><li><span>✓</span>N+1 query problems</li><li><span>✓</span>Missing database indexes</li><li><span>✓</span>Untested critical paths</li><li><span>✓</span>Laravel conventions vs. workarounds</li></ul></div></div>
 <h3 class="h3">Sound familiar?</h3><div class="sit"><div><span class="nn">01</span><p>A previous developer has gone unresponsive and you need someone to pick up an existing Laravel application.</p></div><div><span class="nn">02</span><p>An old Laravel version (5.x or 6.x) needs upgrading to a current LTS release without breaking what’s already in production.</p></div><div><span class="nn">03</span><p>The codebase technically runs but has no tests, no documentation, and nobody left who understands how it works.</p></div><div><span class="nn">04</span><p>Performance has quietly degraded as your data volume grew, and nobody’s sure why.</p></div></div></div>
</section>

<section class="lsec rv " id="performance" aria-labelledby="h-performance"><!-- DYNAMIC: $landing->sections[3] (tiles) -->
 <div class="lw"><p class="eyb"><span>04 · Quality</span></p><h2 id="h-performance">Performance &amp; Security: What I Actually Check</h2><div class="pgrid"><article class="pc"><span class="fi"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="12" cy="5.5" rx="8" ry="3"/><path d="M4 5.5v13c0 1.7 3.6 3 8 3s8-1.3 8-3v-13M4 12c0 1.7 3.6 3 8 3s8-1.3 8-3"/></svg></span><div><h3>Query performance</h3><p>N+1 query problems are the most common performance killer in Laravel apps: a page that looks fine with 10 test records can silently generate hundreds of queries on real production data. I check for this specifically, along with missing indexes on frequently-queried columns.</p></div></article><article class="pc"><span class="fi"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 6h16M4 12h10M4 18h6"/><circle cx="18" cy="16" r="3"/></svg></span><div><h3>Queue &amp; job design</h3><p>Anything that shouldn’t block a user’s request (emails, report generation, bulk imports, notifications) goes through Laravel’s queue system, monitored with Horizon, so a slow background task never makes your app feel slow.</p></div></article><article class="pc"><span class="fi"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 3 4 6v6c0 5 3.5 8 8 9 4.5-1 8-4 8-9V6z"/><path d="m9 12 2 2 4-4"/></svg></span><div><h3>Security baseline</h3><p>CSRF protection, input validation, SQL injection prevention through Eloquent’s query builder rather than raw queries strung together with user input, and rate limiting on public endpoints: the fundamentals that get skipped under deadline pressure.</p></div></article><article class="pc"><span class="fi"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M13 2 4 14h7l-1 8 9-12h-7z"/></svg></span><div><h3>Caching strategy</h3><p>Redis-backed caching for anything expensive to compute repeatedly, applied selectively, because caching the wrong thing creates stale-data bugs that are often worse than the performance problem it was meant to solve.</p></div></article></div></div>
</section>

<section class="lsec rv " id="process" aria-labelledby="h-process"><!-- DYNAMIC: $landing->sections[4] (steps) -->
 <div class="lw"><p class="eyb"><span>05 · Process</span></p><h2 id="h-process">My Laravel Development Process</h2><p class="lead nar">Every engagement follows the same structure, whether it’s a new build or a takeover project.</p>
 <ol class="hsteps" style="--n:5"><li><span class="sn">01</span><b>Map the goals</b><p>I map your business goals, user roles, integrations and success metrics before writing any code, so there are no surprises mid-sprint.</p></li><li><span class="sn">02</span><b>Design upfront</b><p>Database schema, API contracts, queue strategy and the security model are documented, with tenant structure and permission layers defined before the first migration runs.</p></li><li><span class="sn">03</span><b>Build in iterations</b><p>Git-based version control, code reviews where needed, and demos every one to two weeks depending on project size: working features, not slide decks.</p></li><li><span class="sn">04</span><b>Test critical paths</b><p>Automated tests cover authentication, payments, lead routing and payroll calculations where relevant, with manual QA catching edge cases before anything touches production data.</p></li><li><span class="sn">05</span><b>Deploy &amp; hand over</b><p>I deploy to your VPS, AWS or preferred host, configure queues and scheduled jobs, and deliver documentation plus a walkthrough so your team can operate and extend the system.</p></li></ol></div>
</section>

<section class="lsec rv " id="stack" aria-labelledby="h-stack"><!-- DYNAMIC: $landing->sections[5] (stack) -->
 <div class="lw"><p class="eyb"><span>06 · Stack</span></p><h2 id="h-stack">Tech Stack I Use With Laravel</h2><div class="stk"><div><b>Laravel &amp; PHP 8+</b><span>Modern syntax, strict typing, current LTS releases</span></div><div><b>MySQL</b><span>Optimized schemas, indexing, read patterns for CRM-scale data</span></div><div><b>Redis</b><span>Caching, sessions and queue backends</span></div><div><b>Laravel Horizon</b><span>Monitored queue workers for emails, imports, jobs</span></div><div><b>Larastan / PHPStan</b><span>Static analysis, especially for inherited codebases</span></div><div><b>Laravel Telescope</b><span>Request, query and job monitoring while debugging</span></div><div><b>Sanctum / Passport</b><span>Token auth for SPAs, mobile apps, partner APIs</span></div><div><b>Blade &amp; Vue.js</b><span>Server-rendered admin panels or reactive frontends</span></div><div><b>REST / JSON APIs</b><span>Versioned endpoints, consistent validation &amp; errors</span></div><div><b>Forge / Envoyer / VPS &amp; AWS</b><span>SSL, backups, zero-downtime deploys</span></div><div><b>Git</b><span>Branch-based workflow, clear commit history</span></div></div></div>
</section>

<section class="lsec rv " id="projects" aria-labelledby="h-projects"><!-- DYNAMIC: $landing->sections[6] (projects) -->
 <div class="lw"><p class="eyb"><span>07 · Proof</span></p><h2 id="h-projects">Laravel Projects Built for Real Business Load</h2><p class="lead nar">Portfolio demos prove syntax. Production systems prove judgment. These are the same three flagship builds on my CRM Developer page, described here from the backend engineering side.</p>
 <div class="prj" style="--n:3"><a class="pj" href="javascript:void(0)"><img src="<?= asset_url('images/banners/multi-tenant-crm.svg') ?>" alt="Multi-Tenant CRM case study banner" loading="lazy" width="1600" height="1000"><div><small>1.5M+ leads · 300+ users</small><h3>Multi-Tenant CRM</h3><p>Tenant isolation is enforced at the database query level, not just in the interface: every query is automatically scoped, so one client’s data is structurally impossible to leak into another’s. Queue-driven imports handle bulk lead uploads without blocking anyone else.</p><span>Read case study →</span></div></a><a class="pj" href="javascript:void(0)"><img src="<?= asset_url('images/banners/ticketing-crm.svg') ?>" alt="Ticketing CRM Portal case study banner" loading="lazy" width="1600" height="1000"><div><small>Vendors · SLA · workflows</small><h3>Ticketing CRM Portal</h3><p>SLA monitoring runs on scheduled jobs that check ticket age against deadlines and trigger escalation notifications automatically. Role-based access control governs exactly which fields each user type (agent, vendor, manager) can see or edit.</p><span>Read case study →</span></div></a><a class="pj" href="javascript:void(0)"><img src="<?= asset_url('images/banners/hrm-payroll.svg') ?>" alt="HRM &amp; Payroll Portal case study banner" loading="lazy" width="1600" height="1000"><div><small>Attendance · payroll automation</small><h3>HRM &amp; Payroll Portal</h3><p>Payroll calculation logic is isolated from the rest of the app and fully unit-tested: the one part of a system where a silent bug means someone gets paid the wrong amount. Audit logging tracks every change for compliance and disputes.</p><span>Read case study →</span></div></a></div><!-- DYNAMIC: $landing->projects (route to case study) -->
